The paper’s first decisive fact is already clear: Reed–Solomon decoding is efficiently solvable up to the Johnson radius, and the paper proves NP-hardness only in a narrow high-error regime. A naive “plant a codeword and add random errors” distribution is therefore not justified by the theorem and would not support Track A. I’m now checking the paper’s Moments Subset Sum/Prouhet constructions for a native Track B family with a genuine compact-vs-mechanical gap.
